# Entry 11: Why form gramian matrix
entries.append({
    "id": str(int(time.time() * 1000)),
    "level": "L2",
    "interface": "gesvdj",
    "instruction": "Why does GESVDJ form the Gramian matrix A^T*A or A*A^T? What is the mathematical relationship to SVD?",
    "context_text": "GESVDJ computes SVD indirectly through eigendecomposition of the Gramian matrix.",
    "code_blocks": [],
    "answer": "GESVDJ Gramian matrix formation:\n\nGramian matrices:\n- G = A^T*A (Gram matrix, n x n)\n- H = A*A^T (Gramian, m x m)\n- Both symmetric positive semidefinite\n\nMathematical relationship to SVD:\n\nSVD: A = U * Sigma * V^T where:\n- U (m x m): Left singular vectors\n- Sigma (m x n): Singular values on diagonal\n- V (n x n): Right singular vectors\n\nGram matrix A^T*A:\nA^T*A = (U*Sigma*V^T)^T * (U*Sigma*V^T)\n      = V*Sigma^T*U^T * U*Sigma*V^T\n      = V*Sigma^T*Sigma*V^T  (since U^T*U = I)\n      = V*(Sigma^T*Sigma)*V^T\n\nWhere Sigma^T*Sigma is diagonal with sigma_i^2\n\nTherefore:\nA^T*A = V * Lambda * V^T\n- V: Eigenvectors of A^T*A = Right singular vectors of A\n- Lambda: Eigenvalues = sigma_i^2 (squared singular values)\n\nSimilarly for A*A^T:\nA*A^T = U*Sigma*V^T * (U*Sigma*V^T)^T\n      = U*Sigma*V^T * V*Sigma^T*U^T\n      = U*Sigma*Sigma^T*U^T\n      = U*Lambda*U^T\n- U: Eigenvectors of A*A^T = Left singular vectors of A\n- Lambda: Same eigenvalues sigma_i^2\n\nWhy this works:\n\n1. Reduces SVD to eigenvalue problem:\n   - SVD is harder (non-symmetric)\n   - Eigenvalue problem easier (symmetric)\n   - Symmetric -> better algorithms (Jacobi)\n\n2. Singular values from eigenvalues:\n   - sigma_i = sqrt(lambda_i)\n   - Eigenvalues always >= 0 (pos. semidef.)\n   - Square root gives singular values\n\n3. Gets one set of singular vectors directly:\n   - A^T*A gives V directly\n   - A*A^T gives U directly\n   - Other set from A*V or U^T*A\n\nGESVDJ implementation:\n\nTall (m >= n):\n  Compute A^T*A (smaller: n x n)\n  Eigendecompose: A^T*A = V*Lambda*V^T\n  Extract: sigma = sqrt(lambda)\n  Compute: U = A*V / sigma (normalized)\n  Via QR: A*V = Q*R, R diagonal = sigma, Q = U\n\nWide (m < n):\n  Compute A*A^T (smaller: m x m)\n  Eigendecompose: A*A^T = U*Lambda*U^T\n  Extract: sigma = sqrt(lambda)\n  Compute: V = A^T*U / sigma (normalized)\n  Via LQ: U^T*A = L*Q, L diagonal = sigma, Q = V^T\n\nNumerical considerations:\n\nAdvantages:\n- Symmetric eigensolve very stable\n- Jacobi method: High relative accuracy\n- Good for ill-conditioned A\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Condition number squares: cond(A^T*A) = cond(A)^2\n- Can lose precision for ill-conditioned A\n- GESVDJ mitigates with high-precision Jacobi\n\nExample:\nA = [3  4]\n    [0  0]\n    [0  0]\n\nA^T*A = [9  12]\n        [12 16]\n\nEigenvalues of A^T*A: 25, 0\nsigma = [5, 0] (singular values of A)\n\nEigenvectors of A^T*A:\nV = [0.6  -0.8]\n    [0.8   0.6]\n(Right singular vectors)\n\nCompute U from A*V:\nA*V = [4.8  0]\n      [0    0]\n      [0    0]\n\nNormalize by sigma:\nU[:,0] = [4.8, 0, 0] / 5 = [0.96, 0, 0]\nU[:,1] = complete orthogonally\n\nFinal SVD: A = U*Sigma*V^T verified\n\nWhy negate (-A^T*A)?\n- Eigenvalues of A^T*A: positive\n- Want descending singular values\n- Negate -> negative eigenvalues\n- Ascending eigenvalues -> descending |eigenvalues|\n- Corresponds to descending singular values",
    "rationale": "GESVDJ forms the Gramian matrix (A^T*A or A*A^T) because its eigendecomposition directly gives singular vectors (eigenvectors) and singular values (sqrt of eigenvalues). This reduces SVD to a symmetric eigenvalue problem, which Jacobi iterations solve with high relative accuracy. The matrix is negated for correct sorting.",
    "tags": ["gesvdj", "gramian", "eigenvalue", "mathematics", "svd-theory"]
})
